invariant: INV-16
file: p256_wide_scalar.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh p256_wide_scalar
reason: the conditional subtraction of n ignores the limb above the four,
  so a Montgomery product that carried past 2^256 keeps its low four
  limbs unreduced when they are below n. That needs a carry out of the
  last reduction round, which random scalars seldom give, so a vector may
  miss it. proof/p256_wide_scalar_harness.c holds reduce_once to a
  reference that branches, for any four limbs and either value of the
  limb above. Each run is a proof, so this belongs to the nightly's
  test-invariants-proof-backed job.

--- old
    uint64_t keep = p256_wide_mask(borrow & (high ^ 1U));
--- new
    uint64_t keep = p256_wide_mask(borrow);
